PROJECT README

Project Title: Menu Driven Singly Linked List in C++

Overview
This C++ program implements a menu driven singly linked list data structure. It prompts the user to specify an initial size and input elements to build the list. Once initialized, it presents a user interface menu to perform dynamic operations such as inserting elements at a given position, deleting elements from a specific position, searching for a key to find its index position, displaying all current elements, and exiting the program.

Code Architecture
The program uses a Node class containing an integer data field and a pointer to the next node. The main function handles the interactive loop, choice selection through a switch statement, dynamic node allocation and deallocation using the new and delete operators, and safe pointer manipulations to avoid null reference crashes.

Time Complexity Analysis
Linked list creation runs in O(N) time where N is the initial size, as it loops N times to append elements.
Insertion operation runs in O(P) time where P is the target position, requiring traversal up to the insertion index.
Deletion operation runs in O(P) time where P is the target position, requiring traversal to the node preceding the target.
Search operation runs in O(N) time in the worst case to check all nodes sequentially for the given key.
Display operation runs in O(N) time to traverse and print every node from head to tail.
Space complexity is O(N) because memory is dynamically allocated for N nodes.

